---
/**
 * 01 — Hero. The most important section in the build.
 *
 * One job, one second: say that this shop keeps selling when the lights go out.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHY THERE IS NO LONGER A VIDEO
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * The kit's gate for this section reads: "Screenshot the hero with the <video>
 * element deleted. Still a finished, confident composition? If not, the video
 * is carrying the design and the design is wrong."
 *
 * The honest answer, once the client said the video was bad and the
 * screenshots were re-read: the video was not carrying the design, it was
 * SUBSTITUTING for one. Measured, at 1440 it was an unreadable dark smear
 * occupying 60% of the frame; at 375 the scrim swallowed it so completely that
 * the mobile hero — the viewport this audience actually uses — was a flat dark
 * field with a 107 KB download attached to it. It failed the section's own
 * thesis ("handled as material rather than as wallpaper") and the salvage
 * work (crop out the burned-in logo and captions, blur, regrade) had already
 * removed everything that made it material.
 *
 * So the footage is gone. The kit's COMPOSITION rule, though, was right and is
 * kept exactly: type in the left columns, a window in the right columns,
 * cropped by the viewport edge so it reads as a view into something larger
 * rather than a card floating on a background. Only the material inside the
 * window changed.
 *
 * The video is one command from returning if a purpose-shot clip ever lands:
 * scripts/build-hero-video.sh, against hero_vid.MOV, both still in the repo.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHAT IS IN THE WINDOW INSTEAD: the till, mid-sale.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * The headline makes a claim. The window proves it, using the only mechanism
 * that can — by doing it. On load the hero performs a power cut ONCE: the room
 * steps down a value, the connection chip flips to Offline, and the till rings
 * up one more item anyway, the total climbing while the page sits in the dark.
 * That is the entire product argument, performed, in the one place the kit
 * sanctions an orchestrated moment.
 *
 * It replaces the scrim beat the kit specified (§2A's "+600ms scrim 1.0 ->
 * 0.82, the lights coming up"), which died with the video. Same job, same
 * position in the sequence, opposite direction: the lights go DOWN and the one
 * thing that matters stays lit.
 *
 * MOTION COMPLIANCE — this is not a looping ambient animation. It fires once,
 * on load, and settles. What it settles INTO is the offline state, deliberately:
 * a lit till with a queued sale in a dark room is the single frame that says
 * the most about this product. Afterwards the control stays live, so the
 * reader can throw it back and forth by hand — which is what the motion skill
 * means by "a working instrument rather than a brochure".
 *
 * NO-JS / REDUCED MOTION — every state string ships in the markup twice and is
 * switched by CSS off one attribute on the section. The script writes no text,
 * so with JS off the till renders complete and correct in its online state
 * (three lines, D 2,290, all synced). Nothing is stranded and nothing shifts:
 * the queued line's box is reserved whether or not it is shown.
 *
 * THE LINE ITEMS are illustrative, and the only thing on this page that is.
 * They claim nothing about Contekai — a till showing a sale is a depiction of
 * the product doing its job, not a statistic about the business — but the
 * vocabulary is real (Cash / Mobile money / Bank transfer and the dalasi
 * format both come from CONTEXT.md §4's inventory of the actual product), and
 * the arithmetic is hand-checked: 1250 + 780 + 260 = 2290, + 90 = 2380.
 * Swap this for a real 2x screenshot when the client supplies one; that asset
 * is already on the open list in PROGRESS.md.
 */
import Eyebrow from "../ui/Eyebrow.astro";
import Button from "../ui/Button.astro";

/** A line on the till. `late` marks the one rung up during the power cut. */
interface Line {
  item: string;
  qty: number;
  amount: number;
  late?: boolean;
}

const lines: Line[] = [
  { item: "Rice 25kg", qty: 1, amount: 1250 },
  { item: "Cooking oil 5L", qty: 2, amount: 780 },
  { item: "Sugar 1kg", qty: 4, amount: 260 },
  { item: "Bread", qty: 6, amount: 90, late: true },
];

/** `D 1,250`. The dalasi format from CONTEXT.md §1, not a locale guess. */
const dalasi = (n: number): string => `D ${n.toLocaleString("en-GB")}`;

const sum = (ls: Line[]): number => ls.reduce((t, l) => t + l.amount, 0);

/* Both totals are computed, never typed, so the two states cannot drift apart
   from the lines above them. */
const totalOnline = dalasi(sum(lines.filter((l) => !l.late)));
const totalOffline = dalasi(sum(lines));
---

<section
  class="hero"
  id="top"
  data-section="hero"
  data-ground="dark"
  aria-labelledby="hero-title"
>
  {/*
    The room. --ink-800 over the section's own --ink-900 ground: opacity 1 is
    lights on, 0 is lights out. An opacity move on a spare layer rather than a
    background-color animation, so the beat stays on the compositor and the
    motion skill's "transform and opacity only" rule holds.
  */}
  <div class="hero__room" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <div class="hero__inner">
    <div class="hero__type">
      <div class="mask">
        <Eyebrow index="01" class="hero__eyebrow">Works offline</Eyebrow>
      </div>

      {/*
        One <h1>, three lines, authored breaks — and two sets of them, because
        the kit's break is physically impossible on a phone.

        Measured: "when the lights" renders at 7.75x its own font-size. At the
        mobile floor of 2.75rem that is 341px, and a 375px screen offers 335px
        between the gutters. Six pixels short, so the browser re-broke it and
        orphaned "lights" on a line of its own — the ungraceful reflow the
        section spec warns about, present since the first build.

        Below 480px the phrase therefore breaks a word earlier. "when the /
        lights go out." is the only three-line arrangement that fits; every
        other split needs more width than the widest phone has.

        Exactly one variant is ever in the accessibility tree: the other is
        display: none, which removes it from the name computation, so
        aria-labelledby still resolves to one clean sentence. No per-character
        splitting anywhere, and the mask wrappers carry no aria attributes.
      */}
      <h1 id="hero-title" class="hero__title">
        <span class="hero__break hero__break--wide">
          <span class="mask"><span class="hero__line">Keep selling</span></span>
          <span class="mask"><span class="hero__line">when the lights</span></span>
          <span class="mask"><span class="hero__line">go out.</span></span>
        </span>
        <span class="hero__break hero__break--narrow">
          <span class="mask"><span class="hero__line">Keep selling</span></span>
          <span class="mask"><span class="hero__line">when the</span></span>
          <span class="mask"><span class="hero__line">lights go out.</span></span>
        </span>
      </h1>

      <p class="hero__sub t-body-lg">
        Point of sale and stock control for shops in The Gambia. Every sale,
        every item, every dalasi, online or off.
      </p>

      <div class="hero__actions">
        {/* Same label as the header CTA. That repetition is correct. */}
        <Button variant="primary" href="/signup">Start 7 days free</Button>
        <Button variant="quiet" href="#pricing" icon="arrow-right">
          See pricing
        </Button>
      </div>

      <p class="hero__trust t-small">
        No card needed. Pay with Wave when you're ready.
      </p>
    </div>

    {/*
      The window. Square right corners and flush to the viewport edge: it is a
      screen cropped by the frame, not a card floating on the ground. No
      shadow (invisible on dark and pure paint cost), no perspective tilt, no
      blur, no glass — that combination is the mockup-in-a-hero tell the gate
      rejects by name.
    */}
    <div class="hero__stage">
      <div class="till">
        <div class="till__face">
          <div class="till__bar">
            {/*
              Connection state. The dot is filled on --signal when synced and a
              hollow ring when not, and the word next to it says which — the
              state is never carried by colour alone.
            */}
            <p class="till__conn" data-till-live>
              <span class="till__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              <span class="swap">
                <span data-when="on">Online</span>
                <span data-when="off">Offline</span>
              </span>
            </p>
            <p class="till__screen">Point of sale</p>
          </div>

          <div class="till__body">
            <p class="till__heading">New sale</p>

            <ul class="till__lines" role="list">
              {
                lines.map((line) => (
                  <li class:list={["till__line", line.late && "till__line--late"]}>
                    <span class="till__item">{line.item}</span>
                    <span class="till__qty">&times;{line.qty}</span>
                    <span class="till__amount">{dalasi(line.amount)}</span>
                  </li>
                ))
              }
            </ul>

            <p class="till__total">
              <span class="till__totallabel">Total</span>
              {/*
                Both totals sit in the same grid cell, tabular, identical glyph
                count. The value changes without the box ever resizing.
              */}
              <span class="swap till__totalvalue">
                <span data-when="on">{totalOnline}</span>
                <span data-when="off">{totalOffline}</span>
              </span>
            </p>

            <p class="till__pay">Cash &middot; Mobile money &middot; Bank transfer</p>

            <p class="till__sync">
              <span class="swap">
                <span data-when="on">All sales synced</span>
                <span data-when="off">1 sale queued, syncs when you're back</span>
              </span>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="till__under">
          <p class="till__caption">
            <span class="swap">
              <span data-when="on">
                Connected. Every sale syncs the moment it happens.
              </span>
              <span data-when="off">
                No power, no internet. The sale still goes through.
              </span>
            </span>
          </p>

          {/*
            A real control, not a decoration: the reader can throw the power off
            and on as many times as they like. Both labels ship in the markup so
            the accessible name follows the state without script-written text.
          */}
          <Button
            variant="secondary"
            icon="power"
            class="till__toggle"
            data-till-toggle
          >
            <span class="swap">
              <span data-when="on">Cut the power</span>
              <span data-when="off">Bring the power back</span>
            </span>
          </Button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{/*
  ~0.5 KB gzipped. Flips one attribute on a timer, once, then wires the
  toggle. Every visual consequence of that attribute is CSS.
*/}
<script>
  import "../../scripts/hero-power.ts";
</script>

<style>
  @layer sections {
    /*
      min-height and padding-block come from base.css. Nothing here sets either.
    */
    .hero {
      position: relative;
      overflow: clip;
      align-items: center;
    }

    .hero__inner {
      position: relative;
      z-index: 1;
      inline-size: 100%;
      display: grid;
      /* Tighter on a phone, where every 8px is a row of the till that clears
         the fold. Opens back up at 1024 where the two columns sit side by
         side and the gap is doing horizontal work instead. */
      gap: var(--space-6);
      align-content: center;
      min-inline-size: 0;
    }

    /* --- the room ---------------------------------------------------------- */

    /*
      Lights on. The cut drops this to 0 and the section's own --ink-900 shows
      through — the one step down that the design system sanctions between dark
      surfaces (value, never shadow, never a glow).
    */
    .hero__room {
      position: absolute;
      inset: 0;
      z-index: 0;
      background-color: var(--ink-800);
      transition: opacity var(--dur-stage) var(--ease-out);
    }

    .hero[data-power="off"] .hero__room {
      opacity: 0;
    }

    /* --- type -------------------------------------------------------------- */

    .hero__type {
      display: grid;
      gap: var(--space-4);
      /* No justify-items: start — it sizes children to max-content, which stops
         the headline wrapping and scrolls the page sideways at 375px.
         The measure is what caps this block, not a column span. */
      min-inline-size: 0;
      inline-size: 100%;
      max-inline-size: 34rem;
    }

    .hero__title {
      /* Authored line breaks; balance would fight them. */
      text-wrap: initial;
    }

    .hero__line {
      display: block;
    }

    /*
      display: none, not visibility or clipping — the hidden variant must leave
      the accessibility tree entirely or the headline is announced twice.
    */
    .hero__break {
      display: block;
    }

    .hero__break--wide {
      display: none;
    }

    @media (min-width: 480px) {
      .hero__break--wide {
        display: block;
      }

      .hero__break--narrow {
        display: none;
      }
    }

    .hero__sub {
      max-inline-size: 46ch;
      color: var(--muted);
    }

    .hero__actions {
      display: grid;
      gap: var(--space-3);
      inline-size: 100%;
      margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
    }

    /* The primary takes the full width on a phone: it is the one action that
       matters and it should be a large, unmissable tap target. The quiet action
       stays a text action and sits left under it — stretching a text link
       across the screen makes it read as a second button of equal weight,
       which is exactly what "one primary per viewport" forbids. */
    .hero__actions :global(.btn--quiet) {
      justify-self: start;
    }

    .hero__trust {
      color: var(--muted);
    }

    @media (min-width: 480px) {
      .hero__actions {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: var(--space-4);
        inline-size: auto;
      }

      .hero__type {
        gap: var(--space-5);
      }
    }

    /* --- the two-state swap ------------------------------------------------ */

    /*
      Both strings ship in the markup and CSS chooses. The hidden one is
      visibility: hidden, not opacity alone, so it leaves the accessibility
      tree and a screen reader never reads the till twice.

      Default — no attribute at all, which is the no-JS page — is the online
      state. It is complete on its own: three lines, a correct total, synced.
    */
    .swap {
      display: inline-grid;
      min-inline-size: 0;
    }

    .swap > * {
      grid-area: 1 / 1;
      transition:
        opacity var(--dur-base) var(--ease-out),
        visibility var(--dur-base) step-end;
    }

    .swap > [data-when="off"] {
      opacity: 0;
      visibility: hidden;
    }

    .hero[data-power="off"] .swap > [data-when="on"] {
      opacity: 0;
      visibility: hidden;
    }

    .hero[data-power="off"] .swap > [data-when="off"] {
      opacity: 1;
      visibility: visible;
      transition-timing-function: var(--ease-out), step-start;
    }

    /* --- the till ---------------------------------------------------------- */

    .hero__stage {
      min-inline-size: 0;
      /* The crop. The face runs flush to the viewport's right edge at every
         width — one rule, not two layouts: on desktop the frame cuts it on the
         right, on a phone the fold cuts it at the bottom as well. */
      margin-inline-end: calc(-1 * var(--gutter));
    }

    .till {
      display: grid;
      gap: var(--space-4);
      min-inline-size: 0;
      /*
        A till is a piece of equipment, not a banner: past about 40rem the
        panel stops looking like a screen and starts looking like a stretched
        table. Measured at 1920 it reached 1149px of mostly-empty paper.

        Capped and pushed right, so the face stays flush to the viewport edge
        — the crop is the whole idea and it means nothing if the panel floats
        free of the frame. The dark that opens up to its left on a very wide
        screen is not dead space; it is the room the section is about.
      */
      max-inline-size: 40rem;
      margin-inline-start: auto;
    }

    .till__face {
      background-color: var(--paper-hi);
      color: var(--on-paper);
      border-start-start-radius: var(--r-md);
      border-end-start-radius: var(--r-md);
      overflow: hidden;
    }

    .till__bar {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: space-between;
      gap: var(--space-4);
      padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-5);
      background-color: var(--paper);
      border-block-end: var(--border-paper);
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: var(--fs-label);
      letter-spacing: var(--track-label);
      line-height: var(--lh-flat);
      text-transform: uppercase;
    }

    .till__conn {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: var(--space-2);
      margin: 0;
    }

    .till__dot {
      inline-size: 8px;
      block-size: 8px;
      flex: none;
      border-radius: var(--r-full);
      /* Synced: filled. The word beside it says so too. */
      background-color: var(--signal);
      transition: background-color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out);
    }

    /* Not synced: the dot hollows out. Colour is never the only carrier — the
       label next to it reads "Offline" and the line under the total says what
       that means for the sale. */
    .hero[data-power="off"] .till__dot {
      background-color: transparent;
      box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1.5px var(--on-paper-muted);
    }

    .till__screen {
      margin: 0;
      color: var(--on-paper-muted);
    }

    /* A till is a dense instrument, not a brochure card. The padding here is
       one step tighter than the design system's --space-6 card padding for
       exactly that reason, and it buys back a row above the fold on a phone. */
    .till__body {
      padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-5);
    }

    @media (min-width: 1024px) {
      .till__body {
        padding: var(--space-5);
        padding-inline-end: var(--space-6);
      }
    }

    .till__heading {
      margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
      font-size: var(--fs-small);
      font-weight: var(--fw-semibold);
      color: var(--on-paper-muted);
    }

    .till__lines {
      display: grid;
      gap: var(--space-3);
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
      list-style: none;
    }

    .till__line {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr auto auto;
      align-items: baseline;
      column-gap: var(--space-4);
      font-size: var(--fs-small);
      line-height: var(--lh-small);
    }

    .till__qty,
    .till__amount {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    }

    .till__qty {
      color: var(--on-paper-muted);
    }

    .till__amount {
      min-inline-size: 5.5ch;
      text-align: end;
    }

    /*
      The line rung up during the cut. Its box is reserved in every state, so
      revealing it moves nothing: the panel is the same height online, offline,
      and with JS switched off. (CLS on this page is 0 and stays 0.)
    */
    .till__line--late {
      opacity: 0;
      transform: translateY(6px);
      transition:
        opacity var(--dur-slow) var(--ease-out),
        transform var(--dur-slow) var(--ease-out);
    }

    /*
      No JS, no attribute: the queued line has nothing to say, so it stays
      out of the way and the till reads as a finished three-line sale.
      With the power cut, it arrives.
    */
    .hero[data-power="off"] .till__line--late {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: none;
    }

    .till__total {
      display: flex;
      align-items: baseline;
      justify-content: space-between;
      gap: var(--space-4);
      margin: var(--space-4) 0 0;
      padding-block-start: var(--space-4);
      border-block-start: var(--border-paper);
    }

    .till__totallabel {
      font-size: var(--fs-small);
      font-weight: var(--fw-semibold);
    }

    .till__totalvalue {
      justify-items: end;
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
      font-size: var(--fs-h3);
      font-weight: var(--fw-medium);
      letter-spacing: var(--track-figure);
      line-height: var(--lh-flat);
    }

    .till__pay {
      margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
      color: var(--on-paper-muted);
      font-size: var(--fs-small);
    }

    .till__sync {
      display: flex;
      margin: var(--space-4) 0 0;
      padding-block-start: var(--space-3);
      border-block-start: var(--border-paper);
      color: var(--on-paper-muted);
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: var(--fs-label);
      line-height: var(--lh-small);
      /* Two lines reserved: the offline string is longer and wraps on a phone.
         Reserving the taller of the two keeps the panel a fixed height. */
      min-block-size: calc(2 * var(--fs-label) * var(--lh-small));
    }

    /* --- caption + control ------------------------------------------------- */

    .till__under {
      display: grid;
      gap: var(--space-4);
      justify-items: start;
      /* Sits back inside the gutter the face bleeds through. */
      padding-inline-end: var(--gutter);
    }

    .till__caption {
      margin: 0;
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: var(--fs-small);
      line-height: var(--lh-small);
      max-inline-size: 42ch;
      /* Two lines reserved. The two captions are near enough the same length
         but break at different words, so without this the button below would
         hop by a line every time the power is thrown. */
      min-block-size: calc(2 * var(--fs-small) * var(--lh-small));
    }

    /* Sized by the longer of its two labels, so the button never resizes when
       the state flips. */
    .till__toggle :global(.btn__label) {
      display: inline-grid;
    }

    /*
      With no JS there is nothing to throw, and a control that does nothing is
      worse than no control. The caption stands on its own and the till reads
      as a finished online sale.
    */
    :global(html:not([data-js])) .till__toggle {
      display: none;
    }

    /* --- desktop composition ----------------------------------------------- */

    /*
      The kit's layout rule, kept: type in the left columns, the window in the
      right, cropped by the viewport edge. Only the material inside changed.
    */
    @media (min-width: 1024px) {
      /*
        The type column is sized from the headline rather than by a fraction.
        Measured: "when the lights", the longest authored line, renders at
        exactly 7.75x its own font-size across every width (516px at 66.56px,
        554px at 71.5px, 589px at 76px — perfectly linear, as a single
        typeface at one width axis should be). 8.2x gives that 6% of headroom
        for the metric-adjusted fallback face before Archivo lands.

        Sizing the column off --fs-display instead of off the rendered text
        keeps the width deterministic: it depends only on the viewport, never
        on which font is currently active, so the till beside it cannot be
        nudged sideways mid-load. That is the difference between this and a
        max-content column, which would have shifted.
      */
      .hero__inner {
        grid-template-columns:
          minmax(0, calc(8.2 * var(--fs-display)))
          minmax(0, 1fr);
        align-items: center;
        gap: var(--space-8);
      }

      .hero__type {
        max-inline-size: none;
      }

      /* Belt and braces: with the column sized above, the authored breaks
         cannot be re-broken by the browser at any desktop width. If the
         fallback face ever does run wider, it overflows into the 64px gap
         invisibly rather than wrapping to a fourth line. */
      .hero__line {
        white-space: nowrap;
      }
    }

    /* --- the hero load sequence (motion SKILL.md §2A) ---------------------- */

    /*
      Pure CSS, fired at parse. No JS, so it cannot be stranded by a failed
      module, and there is nothing to strand with JS disabled.

      0ms    lights on, till dark
      80ms   eyebrow    mask-reveal up, 320ms
      200ms  h1 line 1  mask-reveal up, 420ms
      260ms  h1 line 2
      320ms  h1 line 3   (the kit's table enumerates two lines; the headline
                          has three, so the 60ms stagger continues)
      340ms  sub        fade + 12px up, 380ms
      440ms  actions    fade + 12px up, 320ms
      480ms  till face  fade + 12px up, 420ms
      520ms  trust      fade, 240ms
      620ms  line 1     fade + 8px up, 240ms   \  rung up, 60ms apart,
      680ms  line 2                            /  three items in the chain
      740ms  line 3
      840ms  caption + control, fade, 240ms
      900ms  THE BEAT — hero-power.ts flips [data-power] to "off" once:
             the room drops --ink-800 -> --ink-900 over --dur-stage, the chip
             goes Offline, and the fourth line rings up anyway with the total
             climbing behind it. The lights go down and the till stays lit.

      Six elements in the longest stagger chain, 60ms apart, within the motion
      skill's ceiling. Nothing here loops.
    */

    .mask {
      display: block;
      overflow: hidden;
    }

    @keyframes hero-mask-up {
      from {
        transform: translateY(110%);
      }
    }

